More issues identified by LLMs

- mlxsw_sp_port_remove() cancels the shaper work before the port is
  hidden from the PUDE event handler, so a link up event arriving in
  between can queue the work again and have it run against a freed port.
  The fix likely involves ordering the removal of the port from
  mlxsw_sp->ports[] against the event handler, which runs in an RCU read
  side section.

- Port teardown (which runs under the devlink instance lock, not under
  RTNL) is not serialized against timestamping configuration of another
  port. MTPPPC and the parsing depth reference count are global, so the
  two can interleave in a way that leaves another port with timestamping
  configured in software but not in hardware. This needs a lock that
  covers the computation of the aggregate configuration, the hardware
  update, and the removal of ports from mlxsw_sp->ports[].

- The runtime reconfiguration of the multipath hash in the router can
  decrease the parsing depth before it writes the hash configuration, and
  its own rollback of that can fail as well.
